## Why This Is Asked
Assesses advanced AWS ABAC, cross-account governance, and policy-as-code in a multi-tenant data lake; targets practical use of IAM Identity Center, PrincipalTags, Lake Formation, and a GitOps deployment flow.

## Key Concepts
- ABAC with session/principal tags
- Lake Formation fine-grained permissions
- Cross-account policy deployment (GitOps)
- Revocation latency and audits (CloudTrail, Config, Access Analyzer)

## Code Example
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": { "Federated": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:saml-provider/AWS_SSO" },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "tenant-A"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake",
        "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake/tenant-A/*"
      ],
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": { "s3:prefix": "tenant-A/" }
      }
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["lakeformation:GetDataLocation", "lakeformation:GetTable", "lakeformation:GrantPermissions", "glue:GetDatabase", "glue:GetTable"],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": { "aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "tenant-A" }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test revocation latency end-to-end? 
- How would you scale this across many tenants and regions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bucket policies, per-prefix isolation, TLS enforcement, and practical test plans.

## Key Concepts
- S3 bucket policies, IAM roles, TLS enforcement, per-prefix access control
- Canary tests to verify allowed and denied actions

## Code Example
```javascript
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::acct:role/TeamARole"},
      "Action": "s3:PutObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::customer-logs/teamA/*",
      "Condition": {"Bool": {"aws:SecureTransport": "true"}}
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::acct:role/TeamBRole"},
      "Action": "s3:PutObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::customer-logs/teamB/*",
      "Condition": {"Bool": {"aws:SecureTransport": "true"}}
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Principal": "*",
      "Action": "s3:*",
      "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::customer-logs","arn:aws:s3:::customer-logs/*"],
      "Condition": {"Bool": {"aws:SecureTransport": "false"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you add a new team without duplicating policy blocks?
- How would you monitor and rotate the IAM roles used here?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes real-world patterns for tenant isolation, scalable quotas, and DR in multi-tenant SaaS on AWS, combining IAM TBAC, API Gateway authorizers, S3 replication, and cross-region resilience.

## Key Concepts
- IAM tag-based access control (TBAC) with aws:PrincipalTag
- Lambda authorizers extracting tenantId from JWT
- S3 bucket policies enforcing tenant-scoped prefixes
- DynamoDB-based per-tenant leaky-bucket quotas
- Cross-region replication (S3) and Global Tables (DynamoDB)
- Route 53 failover for DR

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: Lambda authorizer outline
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const token = event.headers.Authorization;
  const tenantId = decodeJWT(token).tenantId;
  return { principalId: tenantId, policyDocument: { /* allow specific actions on tenant Artifacts */ }, context: { tenantId } };
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you revoke a tenant's access immediately in a shared bucket?
- How would you observe and alert on quota exhaustion across regions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of cross-account IAM and S3 permissions, a common real-world pattern.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-account roles and trust policies
- Bucket policies and principal scoping
- Least privilege and auditability
- Testing: assume role and perform PutObject

## Code Example
```javascript
// Trust policy for cross-account role in Account B
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": { "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A_ID:role/LambdaExecutionRole" },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
    }
  ]
}
```

```javascript
// Inline policy for cross-account role allowing PutObject
{
  "Version":"2012-10-17",
  "Statement":[
    {
      "Effect":"Allow",
      "Action":["s3:PutObject"],
      "Resource":"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-b-name/*"
    }
  ]
}
```

```javascript
// Bucket policy in Account B
{
  "Version":"2012-10-17",
  "Statement":[
    {
      "Effect":"Allow",
      "Principal":{"AWS":"arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A_ID:role/LambdaExecutionRole"},
      "Action":"s3:PutObject",
      "Resource":"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-b-name/*"
    }
  ]
}
```

